<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy Keraunos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ptolemy Ceraunus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy Keraunos was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> who was passed over for the Egyptian succession. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> at Corupedium in 281 BCE, Ptolemy Keraunos assassinated him. The murder prevented Seleucus from uniting Alexander's former territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos.jpg" alt="Ptolemy Keraunos" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stater_of_Ptolemy_Keraunos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
